{"annie|The studio? Pascoe's Studio? …I'll keep the name. And the cat. There's always a cat.":"bd63276f536b9a.mp3","annie|Then we'll keep asking. \"Persistence\" — the sound of it is soft, but the sense is strong.":"44357cd63ff084.mp3","crane|(outside Parliament) You think you've won, don't you, Miss Pascoe?":"a60a38286e8ac8.mp3","crane|A man who claims to be a gentleman should at least admit it. I have nothing left to offer but this: the names of the men who sold me the bad iron.":"6a50dee39fddab.mp3","crane|And what's that? …Don't answer. I can see it. It's those three behind you, isn't it?":"2c84611d8354a8.mp3","crane|I still have my mills, my money and my friends. This was a piece of cake.":"2ee242cecb1aab.mp3","crane|I've come to offer a long-overdue and, I hope, carefully considered apology. I ruined many lives. I won't pretend otherwise.":"079fcf8fde735b.mp3","crane|Miss Pascoe. Your photographs of the Crystal Palace have been admired by many people. I'd like to buy them.":"a9ed96dbc94b76.mp3","crane|They'll be shown at the Exhibition under my name, of course. You'll be paid very well.":"ea703fe10ea138.mp3","crane|You'll regret this, Miss Pascoe. People like you are made to work, not to dream.":"664c0e8d5f0a62.mp3","crane|You're said to be the most stubborn woman in London. It seems you're also the kindest.":"d2a5d259efa56d.mp3","dad|Go to London, love. The lighthouse will still be here.":"b8003b5ced6190.mp3","dad|Never boring, but always tiring. Hello, young man.":"41d0fe104e0c8d.mp3","dad|Where were you yesterday, Nell? You weren't at home.":"18cc725b409945.mp3","dad|Yes. Tonight the light is very important. There are ships out there.":"8a002cb097eb2f.mp3","fenwick|(letter) Annie is fourteen now and as strong as a horse. She wants to learn photography. May I teach her?":"c8b1d30ec17de2.mp3","fenwick|(to Crane) She said no. I said no. The cat said no. Good day, sir.":"7fa1fa2ccbd4f0.mp3","fenwick|A child from a mill, looking at the sea, at the Great Exhibition. That will make people think.":"84984a8c7a4809.mp3","fenwick|And Monday has become our best day. I'm starting to feel unnecessary.":"8dc8ddd9315fac.mp3","fenwick|And the Great Exhibition has asked for your Crystal Palace photographs. Under your name. Your real name.":"760598f110187d.mp3","fenwick|Another one? This studio is becoming a hotel.":"378bc0924581e4.mp3","fenwick|At this speed, in about forty years. …I'm joking. Keep saving.":"b411d2dfb5ddc0.mp3","fenwick|Excuse me, children. Where's the church? And is there a post office?":"1893fcf952a74e.mp3","fenwick|Good. Nervous people are careful. Careful people make good photographs.":"155c918a58ce59.mp3","fenwick|Have you ever photographed water? It never stays still. Good luck.":"c5dcb4bfd30223.mp3","fenwick|He can. But if he touches my chemicals, he's going back to Manchester.":"491a8443ef18e5.mp3","fenwick|Here. Put on this old coat. Chemicals are dangerous for nice clothes.":"73d710fa9fdd9f.mp3","fenwick|I can. Can you hold this? Don't move.":"7ed99690018fa8.mp3","fenwick|I'm leaving tomorrow. I'm going back to London. I'd like to give you something, Nell.":"4b8645593ff147.mp3","fenwick|In a photograph, red looks black. But she can still look beautiful.":"8a9bc8fb3a19c1.mp3","fenwick|It's a camera. It can make a picture with light. I can do it in five minutes!":"6759d73ad728b3.mp3","fenwick|Mr Crane has bought a studio? He doesn't know anything about photography. He must want something else.":"30a321396c74db.mp3","fenwick|My address. When you're older, come to London. I want to teach you.":"a9893a89008596.mp3","fenwick|Nell! You came! I was cleaning a lens when I heard the door.":"e1286705218093.mp3","fenwick|Nell, I'm old. I want to see the sea again. Your sea. I'm going to Cornwall.":"d1454462a2ed7b.mp3","fenwick|Nell, if you finish this order by Friday, you can use my camera on Sunday. Alone.":"de9d41397fa766.mp3","fenwick|People. A tree stays still. A child doesn't. The best photographers are the most patient people in the world.":"c8313afe3ff0f9.mp3","fenwick|Say: \"Pascoe's Studio\". It sounds good.":"50a772d4c7f1b8.mp3","fenwick|The engine is in focus. But look at his eyes. You didn't photograph a machine. You photographed a person.":"99e30e2a3ac2f9.mp3","fenwick|The studio is yours. The cameras, the chemicals… and the cat.":"eeea7257bfea95.mp3","fenwick|Then the truth needs a photographer. You're allowed to use my best camera.":"2c2f6813062494.mp3","fenwick|Then you will. You already live in a house made of light.":"547fb69d0caa83.mp3","fenwick|Then you're the perfect assistant.":"a55e0c7332a65c.mp3","fenwick|There's a railway works near here. They need strong young men. Go tomorrow.":"5b107ac8928cce.mp3","fenwick|This is the most important rule: you mustn't touch the plate. And you have to work fast.":"f155dbbc4b8c02.mp3","fenwick|Today you're going to the market. We need glass plates and silver. Don't pay more than two shillings.":"d2cdcf9f27162e.mp3","fenwick|What are you doing, Nell?":"5bd00e3d971fe5.mp3","fenwick|Would you like to choose your first subject?":"a666a406c87e2c.mp3","fenwick|Yes. You're the noisiest assistant I've ever had. And the best.":"2b8e3d0130c40f.mp3","fenwick|Your gran is a wise woman. Write and tell her you're learning.":"0d65dc1f3d20e4.mp3","gran|(letter) Nell, I wish you were here. The lighthouse is quiet without you. Your dad pretends he doesn't miss you.":"e372c416372c1c.mp3","gran|(letter) Your letter made me cry. Don't wait so long next time, my love.":"0313a0aff1994b.mp3","gran|Me? I only told a story about a mermaid. …Well, perhaps that's how everything starts.":"0104c538673ad7.mp3","gran|Nell is clever and kind. But she's very noisy!":"3070bc3a60f434.mp3","gran|Old? I'm only seventy-two! …Sit down. I've got a story about a mermaid.":"03d00f2df09ee6.mp3","henry|\"Not without merit\"! The man means it's the best thing he's ever seen and he can't bear to say so.":"6857210525b22e.mp3","henry|(later) He told his friends that he'd buy you. He said that every girl had a price.":"83738b7359322f.mp3","henry|(letter) I've just left my father's house. I haven't told him yet. I've already bought a ticket to London. Is there a bed for me?":"c736e0ca1777a7.mp3","henry|(letter) Old? Speak for yourself. I'll write the article. Sam will make the noise. Priya will make them feel ashamed.":"20a587e4b7bb7b.mp3","henry|(reading the list) He's reported to have cheated half the shipyards. Now we know who cheated him. Thank you, Mr Crane. Genuinely.":"c840f2764048d5.mp3","henry|A photographer at war. Nobody has done that properly yet. You'd be the first.":"62f3de0f1dad78.mp3","henry|A story told in photographs! My editor will love it. …I think.":"8f743ff75193aa.mp3","henry|Across the ocean? That's impossible. …Isn't it?":"6e9da2aba8ddc0.mp3","henry|And I'll write it. It's time I stopped being polite to my father's partners.":"914f17b679f13a.mp3","henry|And it appears that the workers at his mill are going on strike. They've had enough. So have I.":"c408f12d816bdf.mp3","henry|Because Sam eats all the food.":"cd785c06b88625.mp3","henry|But big news is coming: there's going to be a Great Exhibition. The whole world will come to London!":"b42d7591da28a1.mp3","henry|But one of the worst mills belongs to Mr Crane, my father's partner. If I were braver…":"48f14254e7b3e2.mp3","henry|By this time next month, we'll have arrived in the Crimea. We'll be living in tents.":"abdd7a85a6edb8.mp3","henry|Every cloud has a silver lining. If the pipe hadn't burst, nobody would have looked at Crane's iron.":"1fb3aa925859f7.mp3","henry|Excuse me. Is this the lighthouse? I'm Henry. My father owns a cotton mill in Manchester.":"10430d8056bded.mp3","henry|Fair. Not generous. Not cruel. Painfully, boringly fair.":"467d24714dd600.mp3","henry|Had I known how much I'd enjoy annoying Crane, I'd have inherited a mill years ago.":"7858b01b09b262.mp3","henry|Had anyone else suggested it, I'd have said no. Since it's you, you're hired.":"aff11e3374f3bf.mp3","henry|Having seen it, we have to tell it. That's all.":"f85d7565e2b714.mp3","henry|His speech was utterly predictable and deeply boring. But his bank was strangely nervous.":"fbd16abec2ad15.mp3","henry|How hard can it be? …Why is the pan on fire?":"ad660472eb438f.mp3","henry|However strange that may sound to my father's friends, it's an extraordinarily good idea.":"63d569ddccc211.mp3","henry|I didn't use to have friends before that summer. I used to read alone all day.":"814fa20c0e9c3f.mp3","henry|I grew up here. I used to look down on these streets from my father's carriage. I never got out.":"62e119a0a74087.mp3","henry|I know. He's paying artists to paint over the truth. My father must know too.":"53d5379864c275.mp3","henry|I read and I write. I like newspapers. And you?":"956498342236f2.mp3","henry|I still am. I've just learned to be afraid with friends.":"8ec75d9cced18e.mp3","henry|I want to write for a newspaper. I enjoy finding stories. I hate counting cotton.":"25fb48d87976ce.mp3","henry|I was wondering whether you'd let me come as the ship's reporter. I'd hoped you might say yes.":"04dfeaf4c6519b.mp3","henry|I'm ill. The doctor says I need sea air. I get up at eight, I walk, I read. Every day. It's boring.":"0b340797c566a1.mp3","henry|I've been working at The Evening Star for six months. I've been writing about fires and robberies.":"7309a193950cc2.mp3","henry|I've drawn up a list of every box. Nell has put together a book of photographs. Sam has backed us up with the ship's records.":"d8fc39018b955c.mp3","henry|I've found a girl who works in Crane's mill. She's the one who wrote to the newspaper. Her name is Annie. She's eleven.":"beada2918e8743.mp3","henry|I've had the windows replaced and the machines guarded. I'm having a school built next door.":"4fb32971d3bfc1.mp3","henry|If Sam stands still. He can't stand still for five seconds.":"a5d88c5cf2c7a8.mp3","henry|If the army had checked the boxes, these men wouldn't have frozen this winter.":"c465a79e1258d2.mp3","henry|In view of recent events, I've written two articles: one for success, one for failure. I'm not burning either until we arrive.":"938152e5b153ac.mp3","henry|It isn't magic. It's science. …It's a bit magic.":"f6342b1fc74b55.mp3","henry|It's the best room in England. My room in Manchester only has a view of a chimney.":"b680791468687b.mp3","henry|Mr Crane, let's not beat about the bush: next time, I'll be writing about your mills. And you won't like it.":"2b7f39efde6381.mp3","henry|My editor is sending me to the front. He wants heroic stories.":"a28e10dbe80874.mp3","henry|My editor says I should write about the mills in Manchester. Children work there for fourteen hours a day.":"8ecf60c8daaeef.mp3","henry|Not that I mind, but did you have to invite the entire village?":"92da514cbbfccc.mp3","henry|Of course I do. And so does half the press in England, now. You've made us all curious.":"2c17b008f6301c.mp3","henry|On the one hand, I want to go home. On the other hand, I'm afraid home will feel too small now.":"97b0393fcbfd46.mp3","henry|One boot, whose owner we'll never know. That photograph says more than all my articles.":"5a0ef1c41fb097.mp3","henry|One day. When my father lets me. …If my father lets me.":"ae9e77bb356990.mp3","henry|Only one thing: my father's face when I come back.":"717d529072601f.mp3","henry|Priya… for better or worse… would you…? Oh, this is harder than facing Crane.":"26281d39faf0d1.mp3","henry|Really? I never play with other children. My father doesn't allow it.":"315c0d0e87be3e.mp3","henry|That sentence will be quoted for fifty years. Not without merit, I'd say.":"a3329ff8ed0fae.mp3","henry|The editor agreed to publish them all. Crane is believed to be furious.":"0ee23da9d618f7.mp3","henry|The fifth of June? That's next Tuesday! I'll bring a present.":"b1c1d7d3db846e.mp3","henry|The message across the ocean. Do you remember, Nell? That lecture, twenty years ago?":"0b0b1d6c7a3d33.mp3","henry|The more people read it, the angrier they get. The more they get angry, the faster things will change.":"143e4679397bce.mp3","henry|The train was late, my bag got lost, and a man stole my hat. I've never been so happy!":"4cf1571593635f.mp3","henry|With a wire, you can send a message from London to Manchester in seconds! It will change the world.":"3b85959e2a7014.mp3","henry|You'd do that? Then I've got no excuse. I wish I'd said yes a year ago.":"2eb55d17288646.mp3","henry|You're right. The iron is cheap and full of bubbles. Guess who supplied it? The provision of these pipes was Crane's.":"1004bf3960500b.mp3","mum|And Sam is tall and funny. Priya is quiet and very smart.":"93339536d1e6a9.mp3","mum|Lunch is ready! There's fish, potatoes and bread. And a Cornish pasty for Sam.":"66256b73e2fef7.mp3","narr|A critic: \"Mrs Pascoe's work is not without merit. Her portraits are, if anything, rather too sympathetic to the workers.\"":"4afb48046ac4a3.mp3","narr|A letter arrived from Manchester.":"a3159bba075261.mp3","narr|A new girl arrives in the village. Her father is a ship's doctor from India.":"1dc7b612da5f68.mp3","narr|A new studio opened on the same street: \"Crane's Photographic Portraits\".":"a4dfcc3fca616c.mp3","narr|A woman on the train: \"Where are you two going? Are you brother and sister?\"":"671ac9f421c955.mp3","narr|After Crane left, Nell's hands were shaking. Sam made her a cup of tea with four sugars.":"3e6ff0bf289f03.mp3","narr|After that, everyone wanted a photograph. Twenty faces in two days.":"41f00999676aff.mp3","narr|After the wedding, a letter arrived for Sam from the Telegraph Company.":"9d4dd58d709b70.mp3","narr|All night, Nell and her dad keep the lamp bright. In the morning, every boat comes home.":"80927f553de0e5.mp3","narr|Annie became the first woman member of the Photographic Society. Nell was in the front row, clapping louder than anyone.":"c909368b27dd63.mp3","narr|Annie came out at nine at night. She was such a tiny girl that Nell almost didn't see her.":"f2fa0013f50462.mp3","narr|Annie looked at the camera and asked: \"Will my picture go to London?\"":"df3ada13ff4e93.mp3","narr|Annie's mother stepped forward. \"Take mine. Everybody knows my face anyway.\"":"e762f8078414e2.mp3","narr|Back at Pascoe's Studio, a letter was waiting. It was from Cornwall.":"e68b4711d06019.mp3","narr|Back in London, a visitor came to Pascoe's Studio. Old, thin and leaning on a stick. It was Mr Crane.":"3caebf49c90cbe.mp3","narr|Back in London, the Royal Society held an exhibition of Nell's photographs of the cable.":"d40d5cc76d9edc.mp3","narr|Be that as it may, Crane refused to give in. He called the strikers \"incredibly ungrateful\".":"34b8aa4987b6d2.mp3","narr|Both photographs were published. People in London cut them out and kept them.":"ecb5f61584f655.mp3","narr|Cornwall, many years later. The lighthouse still shines. On the cliff, four old friends sit on a bench.":"5ab36498e0cbfb.mp3","narr|Crane sold. He left Manchester without saying goodbye to anyone.":"a456c2e8692478.mp3","narr|Crane walked away. For the first time, he didn't smile.":"0c8e1132ec1534.mp3","narr|Crane's lawyers tried to put off the inquiry for months. But the evidence was undeniable, unbelievable, unforgettable.":"6e7f0c5d8696a5.mp3","narr|Dinner was a little burnt. But it was the best dinner in London that night.":"661e6849ade746.mp3","narr|Every Saturday, Nell climbed onto a wall in Hyde Park with her camera. The Crystal Palace grew, photograph by photograph.":"3ee952b4bfbec5.mp3","narr|Fenwick laughed when he said it. But that night, he looked at Nell's photographs for a long time.":"b3ed679fa03a62.mp3","narr|Five years later. Nell is fourteen. The new railway is coming to Cornwall.":"d6806d59a58cce.mp3","narr|From that day on, Nell and Sam are friends.":"fa0ad8733db942.mp3","narr|From the top, the four friends watch the boats come home.":"03dadddfd7ecff.mp3","narr|Henry is back in Manchester. Priya stays with her father. The four promise to write every week.":"b40d1f25a26e18.mp3","narr|Henry started writing that night. He didn't stop until morning.":"313b268c3f18ea.mp3","narr|Henry took Nell to a lecture about the new inventions. A man was talking about the electric telegraph.":"8f4d803ccc3800.mp3","narr|Henry's article came out with eight of Nell's photographs. It made a huge noise in London.":"f747f0711ab053.mp3","narr|Henry's father died that spring. He left Henry his half of the mill in Manchester. The other half belonged to Crane.":"e36718c6844c08.mp3","narr|In 1854, Britain went to war with Russia, far away in the Crimea. Newspapers were full of flags and trumpets.":"9e140a24258fee.mp3","narr|In 1856, the war ended. Hardly anyone celebrated in the hospital. Few of the soldiers had anything left to celebrate.":"2ceba404b784af.mp3","narr|In January 1858, the Leviathan finally floated. Nell's photograph of it was in every newspaper.":"8c15eadd746c14.mp3","narr|In July 1865, the Leviathan left Ireland, paying out cable into the sea like a spider spinning a thread.":"374f2fe5401e24.mp3","narr|In July 1866, the Leviathan sailed again. In light of last year's failure, everything had been redesigned.":"99eb2a2164e892.mp3","narr|In London, Parliament set up an inquiry into army supplies. Henry was asked to give evidence.":"0e7c49ca691cb4.mp3","narr|In London, people began to talk about \"the stone boxes\". Crane stopped going out in public.":"968649f832e24f.mp3","narr|In September 1859, during the Leviathan's trials, there was an explosion. A boiler pipe burst.":"c094c41b78a4ec.mp3","narr|In September, Priya stepped off the coach with a small suitcase and a big smile.":"59dc67bdc4b7d3.mp3","narr|In a wooden hut on the shore, the telegraph operator sat ready. The first message could cross the ocean in minutes.":"e2f60f12b330f9.mp3","narr|In the end, all four went. Fenwick looked after the studio. And the cat.":"0899c6fd82790a.mp3","narr|In the photograph, everyone is smiling except Biscuit the Second, who is eating the cake.":"4da5d2412c6d90.mp3","narr|In the spring, Nell got a fever. Priya stayed by her bed for three days.":"37fa7196833c7b.mp3","narr|In winter, Sam got a bad cough from the smoke at the works.":"0143989ff758ed.mp3","narr|It is said that a nurse walks through the hospital at night with a lamp. The soldiers call her \"the lady with the lamp\".":"52b67095792715.mp3","narr|It was sunny on Sunday.":"db55f801813f75.mp3","narr|It would seem that Crane had sold cheap iron to half the shipyards in London.":"0e80cefc8a3252.mp3","narr|It's autumn. It's windy and cold, and it's raining. A big storm is coming.":"2d1b0833480d1d.mp3","narr|Mr Fenwick's picture shows the lighthouse, grey and silver. Nell stares at it for an hour.":"bb5d4115c780bb.mp3","narr|Nell brought him a basket of food and his favourite pasties from a Cornish baker in London.":"2e53fa79d564fc.mp3","narr|Nell had been thinking about it all night. By morning, she had decided.":"7af59e0fe0f876.mp3","narr|Nell keeps the address under her pillow for five years.":"75f1d54260b9c7.mp3","narr|Nell kept her coins in a tea tin. Every Saturday, there were a few more.":"654bbe732cc94d.mp3","narr|Nell looked at the four of them in the window's reflection. Tired, older, alive. Together.":"87f65ee36b46a9.mp3","narr|Nell packed two cameras, a hundred glass plates and a cart she had turned into a darkroom on wheels.":"af2e274b38bb26.mp3","narr|Nell photographed the exact spot on the sea where the cable had disappeared. Just water. And Sam's back.":"9d99743323e5b1.mp3","narr|Nell put the photograph on the wall. Soon, there would be four faces in it.":"366b4fe374b759.mp3","narr|Nell talked to the boss. He gave Sam a week off. He also asked for a photograph of his wife.":"196ec00282b28b.mp3","narr|Nell took four hundred photographs in the Crimea, many of which showed things no painter had ever shown.":"ec0ee5db667bef.mp3","narr|Nell worked in the day. Priya worked at night. Sometimes they met at dawn and drank tea in silence.":"219666baba3d96.mp3","narr|Nell's first impression of the Leviathan was fear. Her second was love.":"38d115a6fbce9d.mp3","narr|Nell's first photograph showed Sam next to a huge locomotive. His face was dirty and proud.":"c630b71e57a090.mp3","narr|Never before had a ship been photographed while it was being built. Nell came every week, as she had done with the Crystal Palace.":"6e00917d6b00b6.mp3","narr|Nobody in the street wanted to be photographed. Everyone was afraid of losing their job.":"d8bc06787bd8b5.mp3","narr|Now there are four of them. Henry laughs for the first time in months.":"e1a6d2f8a2a717.mp3","narr|On Sunday, the river shone in the sun. Sam couldn't stop talking about engines. Nell couldn't stop looking at the light.":"b15835c9bf99a6.mp3","narr|On the Thames, a ship was growing that was so big it seemed to belong to giants. People called it the Leviathan.":"1c33b81030c4ef.mp3","narr|On the fifth of June, Henry brings a present: a newspaper with Nell's name written on it.":"ef54d3192d9862.mp3","narr|On the first of May, 1851, the Great Exhibition opened. In a corner of the photography hall, a small girl looked at the sea.":"7b18611af80b4d.mp3","narr|On the second of August, twelve hundred miles out, the cable snapped and vanished into the deep.":"e5175a6e22e9b4.mp3","narr|On the twenty-seventh of July, the Leviathan reached Newfoundland. The cable was unbroken.":"d75f482f2c64eb.mp3","narr|One day, a strange man with a big wooden box arrives in the village.":"ed328ea1bd5685.mp3","narr|Priya moved to Manchester for a year. Henry visited the clinic every day. Nobody thought it was because of the clinic.":"1a7bf284101789.mp3","narr|Priya said yes. Then she said: \"It took you twenty years.\"":"d373466a5f0a6a.mp3","narr|Priya's portrait is the first photograph in the village. Her father cries when he sees it.":"aa78d95a55a4f2.mp3","narr|Sam got better. The boss's wife loved her photograph. Two new customers came the next week.":"dd53ad41c24d06.mp3","narr|Sam looked at the boxes again. They all had the same stamp: \"Crane & Partners — Army Supplies\".":"51057ceb040c0d.mp3","narr|Sam was in the engine room. He was carried out with burns on his arms.":"6f61f388ebfe9a.mp3","narr|Sam's arms healed slowly. His anger healed more slowly.":"2d2ddb6fc79d1d.mp3","narr|Some people in the village don't talk to Priya. Nell and Sam do.":"6ceb887687e67d.mp3","narr|Such was the noise of the machines that nobody could sleep. So delicate was the cable that one mistake could break it.":"9f1ef9be63d3b3.mp3","narr|Thanks to Crane's list, the cable for the next attempt was tested inch by inch. Not one bubble.":"c56c540b7e40ae.mp3","narr|That night, Gran tells the story of the mermaid. Nell listens until she falls asleep.":"0258e73b82c11b.mp3","narr|That night, Nell dreamed of a wire under the sea, carrying words like fish.":"cba88592c8c25a.mp3","narr|That night, the four friends talked until the candle burned out.":"8129ff03c0acf0.mp3","narr|That week, Mr Crane came into Fenwick's studio. He smiled too much.":"e2827a8faa1ba3.mp3","narr|That week, Nell spent four hours waiting for one perfect photograph of a cat. The cat moved. She tried again.":"dc78c4b457611a.mp3","narr|The Company had prepared an official message. But the operator turned to Sam: \"Chief, there's room for one more line. Yours.\"":"9cbd8db9c5b80c.mp3","narr|The exhibition ran for six months. On the last day, Annie came, now a photographer herself, with her own camera.":"a54e692d1c9cdb.mp3","narr|The fever broke on the fourth day. The first thing Nell asked for was her camera.":"bea393e888291e.mp3","narr|The inquiry ended Crane's army contracts. But his lawyers kept him out of prison.":"3a03f00f02cd62.mp3","narr|The launch was a disaster. The ship got stuck on the slipway for three months. Sam barely slept.":"366d0a6b5d10ec.mp3","narr|The man laughed and said yes. Nell was getting better at London every day.":"fb2f4b8f6f27a9.mp3","narr|The message reached London in seven minutes. A week later, a fisherman's widow in Cornwall read it in the newspaper and laughed until she cried.":"8f19af8ff24fd0.mp3","narr|The next day, Sam got a job at the engine works. He came back black with soot and smiling.":"9d9175df2677b4.mp3","narr|The ship was crowded and the sea was rough. Sam showed them the engine room every day.":"fac9ff9bbc5839.mp3","narr|The strike ended. Everyone went back to work safe and sound, with shorter hours and a school for their children.":"8f532c75dad571.mp3","narr|The strike lasted six weeks. Nell's photographs of the strikers' families were seen across the country.":"24bb576d280502.mp3","narr|The train was black, loud and full of smoke. Nell had never seen anything so fast.":"ce0fd96c904291.mp3","narr|The wedding was in the little church in Cornwall, next to the pub, opposite the bakery.":"7f412235dd9580.mp3","narr|The woman: \"A girl photographer? How modern!\"":"f5a1d8d959303b.mp3","narr|They sailed home together on Sam's ship. Nobody was left behind.":"35b65d08ddde8e.mp3","narr|They set off at dawn and got to Manchester in the evening. The city was so dark that it looked like night at noon.":"c8dd9af1512fbf.mp3","narr|This is Cornwall, in the south-west of England. There are cliffs, boats and a lighthouse.":"91edb8cdd53a32.mp3","narr|Three friends in London now. Only Priya was missing.":"fb54a804c0e2a6.mp3","narr|Two thousand three hundred miles of cable, coiled in the belly of the Leviathan. Enough to reach from Ireland to Newfoundland.":"7ddc9fa7372a3a.mp3","narr|Whatever the reason, the bank asked for its money back. Crane had borrowed heavily, and the iron scandal had frightened everyone.":"3e6a8d493785f9.mp3","narr|When Henry's article reached London, Crane's company denied sending the boxes. It accused the army of stealing the supplies.":"c15ddbfcdd2edd.mp3","narr|When the train arrived in London, it was raining. The city was enormous and grey.":"bdbde2c0b140da.mp3","narr|When they left, Annie went with them. Her mother asked them to take her to the sea.":"62bc08f2a17bbc.mp3","narr|Years passed. Nell kept asking the Royal Society to accept women as members. They kept fobbing her off.":"9ba68dad59092e.mp3","nell|\"We'll consider it next year.\" Thirty years of \"next year\". I've been given the runaround more than any cable.":"0292dedae7e764.mp3","nell|After seeing all this, I don't know how to photograph a wedding again.":"417fa811e9d67a.mp3","nell|And I want to have my own studio. I love working with light.":"765a751e2ba05a.mp3","nell|Come and see my room! It's at the top of the lighthouse. There are ninety-nine stairs.":"7a40b19077359f.mp3","nell|Hello! I'm Nell. I'm nine. I live in the lighthouse!":"05b351efcd170f.mp3","nell|Henry tends to see stories everywhere. Priya will always help anyone. And Sam… Sam would follow us to the moon.":"c60b52ea004734.mp3","nell|How much is this glass? …Three shillings? That's too expensive. Can you do it for two?":"8b647b7fd81f02.mp3","nell|I can't. But I'm going to London. Anything is possible today.":"8c43390d223762.mp3","nell|I don't think about winning. I think about the men who died, Mr Crane. You don't, do you?":"21d0ea470f56cb.mp3","nell|I remember. I said someone would do it one day. I didn't know it would be us.":"e0edcd51adf4c5.mp3","nell|I want to go to Manchester. I need to see it myself. I can't stop thinking about it.":"363629b120239b.mp3","nell|I was born in a lighthouse. I left school at twelve and I helped my dad for two years.":"dac9ac17adad3e.mp3","nell|I was with Mr Fenwick. We were in the church. It was dark and very quiet.":"2766842520cc9a.mp3","nell|I'm Nell. And you?":"2a3e713890be53.mp3","nell|I'm meeting a new customer on Saturday. Have you ever seen the Thames at night?":"f1a963e99f259f.mp3","nell|I'm watching you. You're cleaning the glass and you're mixing chemicals.":"cc8926adda9a42.mp3","nell|If I hadn't come, nobody in England would ever see this.":"573ab727084e77.mp3","nell|If it rains on Sunday, I'll take photographs inside. If it's sunny, I'll go to the river.":"0394fe650dc7d2.mp3","nell|Look at this photograph from his window. It can't be real. The sky has been painted, and that face is from another picture.":"b9076cf3667b7f.mp3","nell|Mr Fenwick… I don't know what to say.":"8658f401b2ca03.mp3","nell|My dad is the lighthouse keeper. Every night, he lights the lamp.":"24ec7a5f6f2148.mp3","nell|Official photographer. Me. The girl from the lighthouse.":"47d02c2e803e5b.mp3","nell|Photography, trains, telegraphs… People are changing the world every day.":"819b5ebc4a0a79.mp3","nell|Priya… I want you to know something. You're the bravest person I've ever met.":"043e51134f65f7.mp3","nell|So many questions! Sit down. I'll tell you everything. It was raining when we arrived…":"2f6d06e1b431aa.mp3","nell|Stand still. All of you. One last photograph. …Annie, would you take it? I want to be in this one.":"fdaa1f7dffa958.mp3","nell|The church is next to the pub. The post office is opposite the bakery. What's that box?":"96e45c00ede2aa.mp3","nell|This is my family. My dad, my mum, my gran and our dog, Biscuit.":"9e3565460bf35b.mp3","nell|We were walking for two hours. Everybody was shouting and running.":"fd0bb272addf39.mp3","nell|What are you doing in Cornwall?":"9ed689a98a2b54.mp3","nell|What's that?":"a2cbc2c720da96.mp3","nell|What's the most difficult thing in photography?":"e3fc59eff9957c.mp3","nell|You've said \"the future\" eleven times today. I've been counting.":"f70fbddd04e99b.mp3","priya|(arriving from Manchester) Don't bring up the ship now, Sam. I'm in charge. The implementation of my plan begins now: rest.":"f3e521ac01129b.mp3","priya|(letter) I will come! I'm going to study nursing. I think it will happen next year.":"a3350ab25c833e.mp3","priya|(letter) I'm coming to London in September! The nursing school has accepted me!":"458cc59f01d2c0.mp3","priya|(letter) You should rest, Sam. Drink hot water with honey, and you shouldn't work near the furnace. My father says so.":"f066d5ceb34d22.mp3","priya|(on arrival) The hospital is a disaster. No clean water, no beds, no medicine. Someone should have planned this!":"97b1fa5ab0fc34.mp3","priya|(quietly) Sometimes silence is the best medicine. Such is friendship.":"8a49c7cf5d5615.mp3","priya|And Henry was afraid of everything.":"e99c06ba334712.mp3","priya|And I was rather hoping to come as ship's nurse. Five hundred men, a year at sea. You'll need me.":"582b4b827f311d.mp3","priya|And look at us now. Nell has taken three hundred portraits. Sam has built a real engine.":"c1a607e7b19cdb.mp3","priya|Because nobody chooses it. And because I'm stubborn. You know that.":"6ac05e1e74c82c.mp3","priya|Children who breathe cotton dust all day get sick. My father has seen it.":"5f6e32417284c2.mp3","priya|He's forty. Yes, he's a real doctor. He's very good.":"cca6828a95d86d.mp3","priya|Hello. My name is Priya. I'm nine years old. This is my bag, and these are my books.":"2d2102775c5f78.mp3","priya|Her lungs aren't good. However, it's not too late. She needs clean air. Someone has to help her.":"66efc6a1084af9.mp3","priya|How did you find this place? Who pays the rent? Does Mr Fenwick know you've taken over his kitchen?":"4769be65e0c310.mp3","priya|I didn't know. Nobody in London knew. That's why your camera matters.":"c3b908b5b9d104.mp3","priya|I like drawing plants. Nell likes looking at the sea. Sam likes eating.":"f9045d0032165a.mp3","priya|I was terrified. But a sailor had broken his arm, so I helped the ship's doctor. I forgot to be scared.":"eb4cab367eb39d.mp3","priya|I'd love to come. I mean, I'd hate to come. But I'm coming.":"c8885d202aa6f0.mp3","priya|I'd rather you didn't complain on our wedding day, my love. And yes. I did.":"086531c247a6d2.mp3","priya|I'll speak. I'll tell them about every man who froze. Unforgivable is the only word.":"dde4a0bb7f8265.mp3","priya|I'm going too. They need nurses. They say the hospitals are terrible.":"c6edc5b527b12a.mp3","priya|Including my husband, the famous journalist. Not that I'm complaining. …I'm complaining.":"2d71cda5196d88.mp3","priya|It's a map of the world. That's India, and this is England. It's very small!":"dee78d9e52d550.mp3","priya|It's such a sad place. How do people put up with it?":"e4a7eca0eed301.mp3","priya|It's time someone told the truth. It's high time.":"1424bd1ca4ce98.mp3","priya|Me too. I'm going to open a nursing school. There's hardly any training in England.":"46f846c6d0bfee.mp3","priya|Me? There are a hundred nurses, most of whom are braver than me.":"cb54d361d34ce4.mp3","priya|Should he ask me, I'd say yes before he finished the sentence.":"f386144913da11.mp3","priya|So? Where's this famous studio? And why does Henry look so thin?":"7abeabd7211706.mp3","priya|Someone has to speak first. However, it can't be a child. It's not fair.":"ff69df1088db9c.mp3","priya|Then let me tell you my story first. I'd been at sea for a week when the storm started.":"29eeba65ff60f2.mp3","priya|There are many of us with lamps. But I'm proud to be one of them.":"7705e11786ea62.mp3","priya|What I want is for you to get better. What I don't want is to write to your mum.":"bbfef1d1d5c6f3.mp3","priya|What I'd like is for you to stop talking and drink this soup. Then we'll talk about photographs.":"e4cf7e332e9e6d.mp3","priya|What you need is a break, Sam. What you're doing is killing yourself.":"d1968c702926b3.mp3","priya|Wise. A skinny critic shouldn't be fed.":"94cae2f3433427.mp3","priya|Yes, please. With milk and sugar. My mother drinks it with spices.":"7e1187df39bd2f.mp3","priya|You can't think like that. You're here now. It's time to work.":"d96a5b3cd8ea13.mp3","priya|You faint when you cut your finger, Nell. …But I'd love to have you there.":"8ffb8dad53c737.mp3","sam|(at the door) What I promise is this: nobody is getting left behind. Nobody.":"2aecff7602b87f.mp3","sam|(later) Not more than people. Only more than Henry.":"11561955f13c3d.mp3","sam|A box of stones as a present for Parliament. They'll love it.":"fba1bfac5cb2d0.mp3","sam|A pie? You're the best friend in London. …The only friend in London, but still.":"dbe3ca3fe12323.mp3","sam|All the cleverest engineers in England, and the thing that helps most is a pasty.":"6ed59c70c23d9c.mp3","sam|And I've got news too. The Eastern Steam Company has hired me. They're building the biggest iron ship in the world.":"ca50e2b404bfb6.mp3","sam|Be that as it may, I'd appreciate it if nobody told the Company how.":"063e86d090d155.mp3","sam|Be that as it may, the Company only allows one passenger per officer. …Fortunately, I've just been made an officer twice.":"09ec791fa99a8a.mp3","sam|Because iron is beautiful! …Don't look at me like that.":"6452dd94659487.mp3","sam|Can we stop talking about dreams and start cooking? I'm starving. Pass me the potatoes.":"b469dad4d5742f.mp3","sam|Chief engineer. Me. The boy from the fishing village.":"554886864db5db.mp3","sam|Cut the onions, boil the potatoes… and add a lot of love. That's what your gran says.":"246d43fbd4e2a1.mp3","sam|Do I? …Well, at no point have I stopped being ten years old. Ask Priya.":"9fc774b8bc0b50.mp3","sam|Do you remember? We used to sit here and watch the boats.":"acfb81b43aa5d7.mp3","sam|Do you remember? We used to sit on the cliffs and watch the boats.":"eda8886ffa46cb.mp3","sam|Food for thought. Twenty-five years of work… and they give me one line.":"399b11e5ccb5c7.mp3","sam|Hardly any of the stone boxes are left. The army has started checking everything. That's something.":"00adf2ffa0145e.mp3","sam|Hi! What's your name?":"e546ded6976e65.mp3","sam|I love pasties! Can I have two?":"32582cc9a0b4cd.mp3","sam|I remember telling you I hated Manchester. I'll pretend I didn't say it.":"9a175cbcf5422e.mp3","sam|I'm Sam. I'm from the village. My dad's a fisherman.":"0f5ab77e164534.mp3","sam|I'm coming with you! I want to see the engines.":"bb1096e454d926.mp3","sam|I'm from Penzance. But I live here now.":"7fbd49dc288b92.mp3","sam|I'm tired and hungry. And a bit scared.":"5d4db9d2fc57c2.mp3","sam|I've been working on a supply ship for months. It sails to the Crimea next week. So yes, I'm following you.":"c572384ed3515e.mp3","sam|I've filled in the form. I start tomorrow. Try not to blow anything up, Sam. …I'm talking to myself.":"2901fa274406a1.mp3","sam|I've got one clean shirt. I'm saving it for the Queen.":"06c510052b953b.mp3","sam|It's a date! …I mean, it's a plan. A friends' plan.":"2e2a9ee7c28535.mp3","sam|It's down there. It's small and blue.":"9810e168c73612.mp3","sam|Last weekend my dad came home safe. He said the light saved him.":"3d230adbaf9950.mp3","sam|Lazy? He's eating my sandwich!":"25358efe81d358.mp3","sam|Little did they know, when they hired me, that I'd bring my own photographer.":"d3f5492d46602e.mp3","sam|Me? Would you really like to take my picture? …Let me wash my face first.":"6cf274840d0334.mp3","sam|Neither do I want revenge, nor do I want to forgive him. I just want it to stop.":"34756835e1882e.mp3","sam|Never. Have you ever been on a steamship?":"0551cdcc7d9d1d.mp3","sam|No, we're friends. We grew up together. Nell's going to be a photographer.":"cdff5c34f895c0.mp3","sam|Not entirely unexpectedly, the weather is terrible. Not unusually, I haven't slept.":"2e68436d05b10e.mp3","sam|Now and then you have the best ideas in the world.":"654cea47d1fba2.mp3","sam|Rarely do people look at the workers. You always do. That's why your photographs are different.":"e3a9dfdf5bdea2.mp3","sam|Six hundred and ninety-two feet of iron. Five funnels. Paddles and a screw. Nell, it's the future.":"8fff11b71110dd.mp3","sam|Six inches! Six whole inches! What we need now is a party.":"51d9d86642c693.mp3","sam|Tell that to my arms. …No, you're right. Sooner or later, it had to come out.":"f6b585cb72c109.mp3","sam|Thank you, Nell. I'm making a little steamboat. It's a secret.":"e9a0178f01b62e.mp3","sam|That's the most confusing compliment I've ever had. Thanks, Nell.":"f19aa5f87c57dc.mp3","sam|That's the strange thing. The boxes say \"food\" and \"blankets\". But half of them are full of stones.":"d031ae9b95897f.mp3","sam|Then it's this: \"Four friends. One ocean. No distance.\" …It's not Shakespeare, but it's true.":"8cf876c975b1ce.mp3","sam|There's always room for four. That's the rule. It's always been the rule.":"f3551471cf638e.mp3","sam|They want the Leviathan. They want to use her to lay a telegraph cable under the Atlantic. To America.":"3d00a1838b6eb9.mp3","sam|They're building a palace of glass and iron in Hyde Park. I'm starting there on Monday!":"aefa35722022b4.mp3","sam|Thirty miles an hour! Can you believe it?":"11891798a6fa2d.mp3","sam|Thirty years of friendship and I'm still at the back. Fine. I'll stand on a chair.":"2481c26848cddb.mp3","sam|Three hundred boxes. Two hundred and twelve with stones. Inexcusable. Can I go now?":"597d84b199ba7f.mp3","sam|Two and a half miles deep. We tried to grab it nine times. All things considered… we've failed.":"78af653695f996.mp3","sam|Well, it worked for five minutes. Then it exploded.":"c43a6e859289fd.mp3","sam|What I need is a ship that moves. …Sorry. You're right.":"8b409f88712bef.mp3","sam|What are you doing this weekend, Nell? I'm going to the river to see the new steamships.":"161e6eba55d17d.mp3","sam|What do you do in your free time, Henry?":"cb1b8dfc647c04.mp3","sam|Will I lose my job if I stay at home?":"3dd1f565dadbae.mp3","sam|With these arms? …Fine. I'll carry nothing. I'll just glare at Crane.":"436e4cc5d96b54.mp3","sam|Wow! Is this our village? …Where's our village?":"9a488b722ecc02.mp3","sam|Yes… and he's at sea now. He wasn't scared yesterday. I am.":"7a0cc7a92cc27a.mp3","sam|You two again. The Crimea all over again. …Thank you. Don't tell anyone I cried.":"9759c5b950cd59.mp3","sam|You've photographed every one of them. In light of that, I think they work harder. Nobody wants to look lazy in a photograph.":"c3358b1461b5be.mp3","sam|…Every cloud has a silver lining. If we hadn't failed last year, I wouldn't have thought of something this good. Yes.":"6145e689dd714b.mp3","sam|…Next year. Yes. Such is my luck that I've got a friend who never gives up.":"6364a3c54bd3a0.mp3"}